# HTMX and PicoCSS Guidelines

This rule provides best practices for using html/template, HTMX, and PicoCSS in the project.

## Server-Side Integration
- Use html/template for server-side rendering
- Implement encoding/json for JSON responses
- Enable CSRF protection
- Follow Go's error handling patterns
- Implement graceful server shutdown
- Use Go modules

## HTMX Core Concepts
- Default hx-swap is 'innerHTML'
- Use semantic HTML5 elements
- Leverage HTMX extensions when needed
- Enable hx-boost for full page navigation
- Implement proper request handling
- Use context for cancellation/timeouts

## When to Use HTMX

### HTMX is Preferred for:
- Small UI updates (toggles, counters)
- Immediate form validation
- Partial content updates
- Interactive features (same page context)

### Use Regular Forms for:
- Major state transitions
- Full page template returns
- Page context changes
- Full page refresh needs

## Common Patterns

### Page Transitions (Regular Forms)
```html
<form method="post" action="/action">
    <button type="submit">Do Action</button>
</form>
```

### Partial Updates (HTMX)
```html
<div id="user-prefs">
    <form hx-post="/preferences" 
          hx-target="#user-prefs">
        ...
    </form>
</div>
```

### Form Validation (HTMX)
```html
<form hx-post="/validate" 
      hx-target="this">
    ...
</form>
```

### Modals/Overlays (HTMX)
```html
<div id="modal-container"></div>
<button hx-get="/modal" 
        hx-target="#modal-container">
    Open Modal
</button>
```

## Best Practices
- Avoid full page templates in HTMX responses (except body target)
- Create dedicated partial templates
- Use specific container IDs
- Place modals/overlays outside main content
- Return minimal validation markup
- Use regular forms for major transitions
- Prioritize user experience in HTMX vs. regular form decisions 